      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/fa4b5ffe6c0abfa7ca5583fe2304d1acc2d35fa5d8949bf9526b2efd296316cd.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(-2),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-2.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-3,0].$ Notice that $x=-2$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/63ea83d99ec5e3ba226fea092134d8289e0e88f0b0d426a99a9b69f7fe5abe75.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-3,0)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-(-2)}{0-(-3)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
